+// coco_agi ORACLE INSTRUMENTATION (P4.1) -- DETERMINISTIC VIRTUAL CLOCK.
+//
+// ★★ WHY THIS EXISTS. The in-game timer is driven by getTotalPlayTime(), i.e. by REAL
+// WALL-CLOCK MILLISECONDS. VM_VAR_SECONDS/MINUTES/HOURS/DAYS (vars 11-14) are computed from
+// it in inGameTimerUpdate() below, so their values depend on how fast the host happened to
+// run. Anything a game's LOGIC derives from them inherits that nondeterminism.
+//
+// Measured, not assumed (P4.1): two runs of the UNMODIFIED oracle over kq4dem, one on an idle
+// machine and one under load, diverge at vars 11, 72 and 73 -- 11 being the clock and 72/73
+// being kq4dem's own script values derived from it. That reproduces the P0.3 finding exactly.
+//
+// ★★ THE ALTERNATIVE WAS TO EXCLUDE 11/72/73 FROM THE STATE DIFF, AND THAT IS THE WRONG
+// TRADE. 72 and 73 are ordinary game variables; excluding them puts real interpreter state
+// permanently outside the gate to work around a harness problem. Removing the nondeterminism
+// at its source keeps them under test. (CLAUDE.md §2O.1 in spirit: fix the harness, never
+// weaken the baseline.)
+//
+// The clock advances 25 ms per main-loop iteration -- exactly the unit cycle.cpp's pacing gate
+// already counts in ("Our cycle counter runs at 25 milliseconds", cycle.cpp) -- so the
+// cycle:time relationship is the one the engine already assumes, now exact instead of
+// approximate. Nothing else about timing behaviour changes.
+//
+// ★ §2.1: this is a DEVIATION FROM THE ORACLE'S OWN BEHAVIOUR, adopted for determinism. It is
+// not a claim about what Sierra's interpreter did. It happens to sit CLOSER to the CoCo3
+// target, whose clock will be driven by a 60 Hz VBL count rather than by a host clock, but
+// that is a convenience and not the justification.
+uint32 g_oracleVirtualMs = 0;
+
